## `cfl_variant_create_from_reference`

```c
struct cfl_variant *cfl_variant_create_from_reference(void *value)
{
    struct cfl_variant *instance;

    instance = cfl_variant_create();
    if (instance != NULL) {
        instance->data.as_reference = value;
        instance->type = CFL_VARIANT_REFERENCE;
    }

    return instance;
}
```

## `cfl_variant_destroy`

```c
void cfl_variant_destroy(struct cfl_variant *instance)
{
    if (!instance) {
        return;
    }

    cfl_container_release_variant(instance);

    if (instance->type == CFL_VARIANT_STRING ||
        instance->type == CFL_VARIANT_BYTES) {
        if (instance->data.as_string != NULL && !instance->referenced) {
            cfl_sds_destroy(instance->data.as_string);
        }
    }
    else if (instance->type == CFL_VARIANT_ARRAY) {
        cfl_array_destroy(instance->data.as_array);
    }
    else if (instance->type == CFL_VARIANT_KVLIST) {
        cfl_kvlist_destroy(instance->data.as_kvlist);
    }

    free(instance);
}
```

## How the `referenced` parameter is handled

The `referenced` field controls ownership of the underlying data:

- **On creation** (string and bytes): The struct field is set with `"instance->referenced = referenced ? CFL_TRUE : CFL_FALSE;"`. When `referenced` is true, the pointer is stored directly (the caller retains ownership of the memory). Otherwise, a new copy is allocated via `cfl_sds_create_len`, and the size limit against `INT_MAX` is checked.

- **On destruction**: For string/bytes types, memory is freed with `cfl_sds_destroy` only if the data pointer is non-NULL **and** the variant is not referenced. This prevents freeing memory the variant doesn't own. Array and kvlist types delegate to their own destroy functions, and finally the instance itself is freed.

Note that `cfl_variant_create_from_reference` sets the `REFERENCE` type but does not touch the `referenced` field — since `cfl_variant_create` uses `calloc`, that field defaults to zero.

